For each start time $t_0$ and flexibility magnitude $\Delta P$, the feasibility of providing the given $\Delta P$ is tested over a series of durations. \rev{The optimisation described in Scenario 2 is run for each duration over an evaluation window clipped to $t_0 \leq t \leq t_0 + \tau + 12$. The flexibility constraint in Equation~(\ref{eq:flex_constraint}) applies only during the service-delivery interval $t_0 \leq t \leq t_0 + \tau$. The additional $12$ time slots form a recovery window, equal to the maximum delay tolerance of any IT workload, during which all physical, thermal, storage and workload-completion constraints continue to apply but the grid-power deviation is no longer enforced, so the grid power draw may vary relative to the optimised baseline.} The flexibility provision is only deemed feasible if the DC can operate without breaching any constraints in the $12$ slot recovery window. All of the IT workload initially scheduled for completion in the recovery window is considered inflexible so that the DC can go back to the operating conditions defined in Scenario 2 after the recovery time.

If the result for a given duration $\tau$ at timeslot $t_0$ and flexibility magnitude $\Delta P$ is feasible, the DC can provide that level of flexibility for the given duration. The same feasibility test is then repeated for a longer duration. If the result is infeasible, the test is run for a shorter duration. The process is repeated until the maxima $\tau(t_0, \Delta P)$ is found. The duration is modified using binary-search, so that $\mathcal{O}(\log \tau)$ feasibility checks are required per scenario. This efficient approach minimises computational overhead. The assessment is repeated across a grid of start times spanning the operating day and across a set of upward and downward flexibility magnitudes.

Lastly, the cold aisle temperature range is extended from $18$--$22.5 ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ to $18$--$23^\circ\mathrm{C}$ in Scenario 3. \rev{This wider range is used because Scenario 3 assesses flexibility provision in response to an explicit grid request, rather than routine cost-only operation: the $0.5^\circ\mathrm{C}$ increase provides limited additional thermal headroom during the flexibility event while remaining conservative relative to both the $27^\circ\mathrm{C}$ upper limit proposed by ASHRAE \cite{tc2016data} and the $23$--$24^\circ\mathrm{C}$ cold-aisle set points commonly used in modern facilities.}

